---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import TireSize from '@/components/tools/calc2/TireSize.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="tire-size-calculator">
  <TireSize client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the tire size calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      A metric tire size gives the width in millimetres, the sidewall height as a percentage of the width,
      and the rim diameter in inches. The tire size calculator turns that into the overall diameter, then
      compares two sizes.
    </p>
    <pre><code>sidewall       = width × aspect ratio ÷ 100            (mm)
diameter       = rim × 25.4 + 2 × sidewall              (mm)
circumference  = π × diameter
turns per mile = 1,609,344 mm ÷ circumference
true speed     = speedometer × new diameter ÷ old diameter</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      225/45R17: the sidewall is 225 × 0.45 = 101.25 mm and the diameter 17 × 25.4 + 2 × 101.25 = 634.3 mm
      (24.97 in). 245/40R18: 98 mm sidewall and 653.2 mm (25.72 in). The new tire is 2.98% larger, so at an
      indicated 60 mph the car does 61.8 mph, and the odometer under-reads by the same 2.98%.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The results use the nominal size. Real tires of one size differ by a few millimetres between brands, and wear takes off up to 8 mm of tread depth.</li>
      <li>Keep the load index and speed rating at least as high as the original, and check that the new size clears the suspension and arches.</li>
      <li>Flotation sizes such as 33x12.50R15 already give the diameter in inches: 33 in.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
